WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.520
Speaker 1: A very interesting new study finds&nbsp;
that Americans on average die younger in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:11.460
conservative and red states. Now, this won't&nbsp;
be a surprise to some of you who know some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:16.440
of the underlying data here. But let's start&nbsp;
with the study. As reported by The Guardian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:24.000
more liberal policies on environment, gun safety,&nbsp;
labor, economic taxes and tobacco taxes associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:31.560
with lower mortality. The authors of the study&nbsp;
write simulations indicate that changing all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:38.100
policy domains in all states to a fully liberal&nbsp;
orientation could have saved over 171,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:47.460
lives in 2019. Changing to a fully conservative&nbsp;
orientation could cost more than 217,000 lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:53.400
The study was published in PLoS One, PLoS One,&nbsp;
PLoS One. I always forget how it's pronounced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.400 --> 00:01:00.660
which is a science journal. The authors are from&nbsp;
Syracuse, Syracuse University, Harvard, VCU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:05.520
University of Washington, University of Texas at&nbsp;
Austin and University of Western Ontario, Canada.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:12.720
I have to tell you, this really isn't surprising.&nbsp;
There is a very interesting metric, and when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.760
talk about the United States, sometimes it's said,&nbsp;
well, the United States could really be divvied up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:25.800
into six different regions. That would be as&nbsp;
big as many European countries or 12 different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:33.540
regions. I have seen there is a metric called&nbsp;
the Human Development Index. This metric takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:43.200
into account health, education, income and life&nbsp;
expectancy to give us a number essentially below&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:49.380
zero and one one being very high and zero being&nbsp;
very low. And when you look around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:56.580
you see maybe not like a hugely surprising&nbsp;
list of countries at the top of the list. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:03.060
for example, countries with a very, very high&nbsp;
HDI human development index. Switzerland, Norway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:10.080
Iceland. Hong Kong. Australia. No real surprises&nbsp;
there. Right there with them. Denmark. Sweden.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:16.200
Ireland. Germany. Netherlands. Finland. Singapore,&nbsp;
Belgium. New Zealand. Canada. You start to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:24.120
trends in the types of places that these are. You&nbsp;
can assign values just like you can to a country,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:30.480
to a state. And one of the things that you will&nbsp;
see, which are totally separate from this study&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:37.920
that we're talking about here, is that the states&nbsp;
with the highest HDI is also are overwhelmingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:45.060
blue states. You've got at the top, Massachusetts,&nbsp;
Connecticut, Minnesota, New Jersey, New Hampshire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:56.100
Colorado, D.C., North Dakota, Washington, Hawaii,&nbsp;
New York, Maryland, Vermont. So we are 1314 in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:06.240
With one red state and one purple state of New&nbsp;
Hampshire. This is not new information to many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:14.640
us. Now, what's an important question in general,&nbsp;
is the life expectancy specifically? Do they die&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:22.680
younger because they're poorer and they're poorer&nbsp;
due to Republican economic policy? Or do they die&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:29.340
younger because of the direct effect of Republican&nbsp;
social and other policies? The answer pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:35.100
clearly seems to be a sort of all of the above&nbsp;
type of thing. Going back to the article a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:42.180
bit, especially strong associations were observed&nbsp;
between certain domains and specific causes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:48.840
death, between gun safety and suicide mortality&nbsp;
among men. Between labor and alcohol induced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:57.540
mortality. Between both the economic tax and&nbsp;
tobacco tax. And cardiovascular mortality. Very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:03.000
very interesting. And the study authors also note&nbsp;
that American life expectancy as a whole is lower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:09.660
than most high income countries falling between&nbsp;
Cuba and Albania. One of the things that sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:16.320
will come up on the show is I want to move to a&nbsp;
country with a much higher standard of living.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:24.480
And one of the things to understand is that a lot&nbsp;
of times the folks saying that are in states with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:29.640
low standards of living, it's difficult to move to&nbsp;
a lot of the countries on the list I showed you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:34.980
But it's much easier, relatively. It's not free.&nbsp;
There's still cost. And sometimes those states are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:40.800
more expensive. You can much more easily move to&nbsp;
a state in the U.S. with an equivalent standard of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:45.480
living. To many of those European countries,&nbsp;
because the United States is so stratified.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:54.180
And the difference between Massachusetts and&nbsp;
Mississippi on every indicator could not be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.180 --> 00:05:00.060
different. So interesting study, not surprising&nbsp;
information, but interesting to see it laid out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:04.920
so cleanly in terms of the data. We'll link to the&nbsp;
study so you can take a look at it for yourselves.

